course: dictionary
lesson: 656
title: Bagel in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bagel is a food word you may meet in shopping, breakfast, or maths problems. Its context gives clues about its meaning. «A toasted bagel with cream cheese» shows that a bagel is a kind of bread that can be eaten with a spread.
explain: Look for words that describe the bagel. «A sesame bagel» tells you it can have seeds on top. The word ring-shaped in the dictionary tells you what a bagel looks like: a round shape with a hole in the middle.
explain: Maths problems use bagels too, such as sharing a dozen bagels between friends. Ask yourself what the question wants you to find, then use the clues in the sentence to work it out.
fact: A bagel is a ring-shaped bread roll with a hole in the middle.
fact: A bagel is boiled before it is baked, so it is chewy.
fact: Bagels are a common food for breakfast or lunch in the USA, Canada, the UK and other countries.
fact: A «toasted bagel» has been heated until its outside is golden and crisp.
fact: Bagels can be plain or have toppings, such as «a sesame bagel» with seeds on top.
exercise: What is a bagel? | * A ring-shaped bread roll with a hole in the middle | A flat, round biscuit | A small cake with icing | A long stick of cheese
exercise: Why is a bagel chewy? | * It is boiled before it is baked | It is frozen in ice | It is fried in hot oil | It is made with chocolate
exercise: In «a sesame bagel», what does sesame tell you? | The bagel is filled with jam | The bagel is very large | The bagel is cold and frozen | * The bagel has seeds on top
test: «A boy in Ohio tried to cut a bagel by holding it in his palm.» What does this sentence teach us? | * Holding a bagel in your hand is not a safe way to cut it | Bagels are always soft and easy to cut | Ohio is famous for its bagels | Palms are the best tools for cutting bread
test: What does a bagel have that an ordinary bread roll does not? | A sweet, sugary icing | * A hole in the middle | Ham and cheese inside | Seeds on top every time
exam: In maths, Sam has a dozen bagels and shares them equally between four friends. How many bagels does each friend get? | 8 | 2 | * 3 | 4
